{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1779167081,"nonce":"KYR7JRL/RJ2X0X3AzlNdcu0YFpY=","message":"## Implementation spec before editing\n\nThe table should stop being RLRMP-shaped. The next slice should make metric columns a small view over metric specs and imported metric values, with a human-facing default set.\n\nDesign stance from the current code:\n\n- Reuse `scenarioMetricSpecs(workspace)` as the typed/source-aware metric catalog, but extend it enough to distinguish table-display metrics from background provenance.\n- Keep imported manifest values available, but label them as imported when no authored metric spec exists. They can fill table cells; they should not imply that Studio authored that metric.\n- Refactor `TrainingRunSummary` away from fixed top-level metric fields as the table contract. It can still expose compatibility fields internally, but the table should render columns from `ScenarioMetricSpec` and per-run metric values.\n- Sort state should use metric IDs, not hardcoded keys such as `velocityRmse` or `holdDrift`.\n\nInteraction model:\n\n1. The run table has fixed structural columns for selection, run label/variant, status, progress/checkpoint, and details.\n2. Metric columns are chosen from task/objective/analysis/imported specs. The first default can be loss plus the highest-value compatible metrics for the current task/imported fixture; the user should not see every manifest key by default.\n3. Metric headers are compact, sortable, and show units/provenance in a tooltip/title or concise secondary text. Avoid a separate always-visible metric metadata dump.\n4. Imported metrics can appear in the table because they are useful, but provenance belongs in an unobtrusive trace/tooltip/detail, not as repeated source labels in every row.\n5. Run details should show missing/unmapped fields honestly without turning into a database-key list.\n\nPrecedent patterns to carry forward:\n\n- ML experiment tables such as Neptune/W\u0026B: configurable columns, sortable metrics, filters/selections, pinned/high-value columns, and compare views.\n- Data workflow tools: show readiness and selected inputs as actions, not as raw collection IDs.\n- Engineering dashboards: units and directionality matter; \"best\" should be a sort/view based on metric semantics, not a hardcoded card.\n\nAcceptance for this slice:\n\n- Training and Evaluate run tables render metric columns from derived metric specs rather than hardcoded RLRMP fields.\n- Sorting works for selected metric columns, including imported metrics.\n- At least one test proves a non-hardcoded metric spec can drive a run-table column.\n- The seeded RLRMP example still displays useful velocity/hold/loss metrics, but through imported/analysis metric specs rather than fixed table columns.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}